        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Pretrained transformer models for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data have rapidly become a standard analytical tool. Geneformer (Theodoris et al., 2023), scGPT (Cui et al., 2024), UCE (Rosen et al., 2023), and scFoundation (Hao et al., 2024) now provide cell representations that can be evaluated zero-shot or fine-tuned across a broad set of downstream tasks. A central premise of zero-shot evaluations is that the model has not seen the benchmark data during pretraining; if benchmark cells are in the pretraining corpus, performance estimates conflate generalization with memorization or distributional familiarity.",
            "block_bbox": [
                123,
                1174,
                1064,
                1363
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    123.0,
                    1174.0
                ],
                [
                    1064.0,
                    1174.0
                ],
                [
                    1064.0,
                    1363.0
                ],
                [
                    123.0,
                    1363.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Pretraining corpora for current scFMs are constructed from CELLxGENE Census (Program et al., 2025), the Human Cell Atlas (Regev et al., 2017), and curated additions, and span tens of millions of cells. Genecorpus-30M (Theodoris et al., 2023) was assembled from publicly available scRNA-seq through mid-2021; the CELLxGENE Census used by scGPT (33M cells) and UCE (33M+ human; 36M cross-species) was a 2023 release. Both corpora draw from the same upstream Gene Expression Omnibus and ArrayExpress deposits that underlie widely cited integration benchmarks, including PBMC 3k (Zheng et al., 2017) and human pancreatic islet atlases (Baron et al., 2016).",
